Print the heartbeat every second, with the clock

The heartbeat printed every 50000 iterations of delay(100), which is 5000
seconds at a 100 Hz tick: it had never printed once.  A liveness marker
that never fires is not a liveness marker, and the boot is killed by the
watchdog after about a minute, so the panel has to show that a minute
passing.

The interval is measured with the clock instead of a tick count, so it is
one second whatever the tick rate is, and the line carries both the tick
counter and the microsecond stamp: the two together say how fast the ticks
run, and the stamp lines the other prints up with the wall clock.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/startup.c b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
index 89618a9..699ddc0 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -398,9 +398,20 @@ static void
 nio_heartbeat_thread(void *arg, wait_result_t wr)
 {
 	unsigned n = 0;
+	uint32_t nio_hb_last = 0;
 	for (;;) {
+		clock_sec_t nio_hb_sec;
+		clock_usec_t nio_hb_usec;
+		uint32_t nio_hb_now = 0;
+
 		delay(100);
-		if ((++n % 50000u) == 0) { printf("nio: hb %u\n", n); }
+		n++;
+		clock_get_system_microtime(&nio_hb_sec, &nio_hb_usec);
+		nio_hb_now = nio_hb_usec + (uint32_t)(nio_hb_sec % 1000) * 1000000u;
+		if ((uint32_t)(nio_hb_now - nio_hb_last) >= 1000000u) {
+			nio_hb_last = nio_hb_now;
+			printf("\nnio: hb %u %u", n, nio_hb_now);
+		}
 	}
 }
 
